# client-billing-settings.query-key-scope-keyed.must-fail.patch
#
# Targets: the FE-3240 behaviour-preserving invariant — the record query key is
# the RESOLVED client id, never the scope key. Two scopes that resolve the SAME
# client (the session's own arm, and an explicit `.for(CLIENT, clientId)`) have
# DIFFERENT scope keys but must share ONE `["client", id, "record"]` cache
# entry and one fetch.
#
# Authored by: developer seat (agent-seat-separation.companion.md — the seat
# that knows the exact line it changed authors the mutant; the prover applies
# it blind, confirms RED, then reverts — never reading src to construct it).
#
# Mutates: client-billing-settings.services.ts loadSettings — folds the scope
# context's id (`scopeContext?.id`) into the record query key. The key is now
# scope-SENSITIVE: the session-own scope (`scopeContext === undefined`, so
# `scopeContext?.id === undefined`) and the `.for(CLIENT, clientId)` scope
# (`scopeContext.id === clientId`) mint two DISTINCT entries instead of
# collapsing onto the shared `["client", id, "record"]` key.
#
# Expected RED: client-billing-settings.read.int.test.ts —
# "AC2 two instances resolving the same client id via different scope keys share
# ONE ['client', id, 'record'] fetch" — `recordEntries` is 0 (both keys now carry
# a 4th scope segment, so neither matches the shared 3-tuple) and
# `ownRecordQueryDataUpdateCount(clientId)` no longer settles to 1.
#
# Apply / verify / revert:
#   git apply <this file>
#   pnpm --filter @upmind-automation/headless test client-billing-settings.read.int   # must go RED
#   git apply -R <this file>
#
